---
import CaseStudyMedia from "./CaseStudyMedia.astro";
import { bloxelsMedia } from "../data/bloxelsMedia";
import BloxelsMeshComparison from "./BloxelsMeshComparison.astro";
const biomes = [
    ["Verdant Lowlands", "Rolling meadows, rounded oaks, and limestone."],
    ["Amberwood Forest", "Wetter hills, broad crowns, leaf litter, and coherent clearings."],
    ["Azure Highlands", "Ridged terrain, exposed rock, sparse trees, and azure deposits."],
    ["Rustwind Desert", "Dry terraces, sand and sandstone layers, and sparse deadwood."],
    ["Frostglass Reach", "Snow-covered hills, conifers, ice formations, and frostglass deposits."],
];
---
<section class="case-section contribution-block" aria-labelledby="bloxels-contributions">
    <h2 id="bloxels-contributions">My Contributions</h2>
    <p>As the solo Systems Programmer / Technical Designer, I owned the C++ systems connecting seeded world generation to visible, editable terrain and custom navigation.</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Developed deterministic five-biome generation with blended terrain, seeded features, and regional underground shapes.</li>
        <li>Built a shared voxel mesher with hidden-face removal and axis-parameterized greedy merging.</li>
        <li>Managed asynchronous chunk lifecycles, nearby/edit priorities, and safe publication of worker results.</li>
        <li>Implemented coarse terrain representations and a custom render proxy that caches unchanged terrain draw submissions.</li>
        <li>Built on-demand voxel A* traversal and visual tools for inspecting search decisions.</li>
        <li>Created configurable block definitions, generation recipes, and structure import/export tooling.</li>
    </ul>
</section>
<section class="case-section" aria-labelledby="bloxels-about">
    <h2 id="bloxels-about">About This Project</h2>
    <p>Bloxels is a solo C++ voxel-world prototype built in Unreal Engine 5.6. I developed deterministic procedural generation across five biomes, editable terrain with asynchronous chunk streaming and multiple levels of detail, optimized voxel meshing, and custom A* navigation.</p>
    <p>The project focuses on keeping generated worlds consistent across chunk boundaries, controlling generation and rendering work, and making complex systems inspectable through visual demonstrations and development tools.</p>
</section>
<section class="case-section" aria-labelledby="bloxels-world">
    <h2 id="bloxels-world">Procedural World Generation</h2>
    <p>The design challenge was to create recognizable regions and varied content while keeping generation deterministic across chunk boundaries. Project-owned seeded 2D/3D noise drives broad climate and classification fields separately from detailed terrain elevation. Configurable biome profiles blend terrain heights; coherent palette transitions carry ground, soil, and rock materials across borders without numerically blending block identities.</p>
    <figure>
        <iframe class="bloxels-world-video" src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/Y6qKaFuA_zU?playsinline=1" title="Bloxels landscape flyover" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share" allowfullscreen />
        <figcaption>A downward-looking flyover of the textured voxel landscape.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="bloxels-biome-gallery" aria-label="Five biome examples">
        {biomes.map(([name, description]) => {
            const src = `/images/projects/bloxels/${name.replaceAll(" ", "")}.png`;
            return <figure>
                <a href={src} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label={`Open ${name} screenshot at full size`}><img src={src} alt={`${name} biome in the generated voxel world`} loading="lazy" /></a>
                <figcaption><strong>{name}</strong><br />{description}</figcaption>
            </figure>;
        })}
    </div>
    <figure class="case-media">
        <a href="/images/projects/bloxels/Seed1337_Preview.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Open the full-size seed 1337 world-generation survey"><img src="/images/projects/bloxels/Seed1337_Preview.png" alt="Seed 1337 survey comparing the five dominant biome regions, surface height, and climate fields." loading="lazy" /></a>
        <figcaption>Seed 1337: dominant biome, surface height, and climate sampled from −4,096 to +4,096 blocks at 64-block intervals. Open the image for the full-size survey.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <h3>Seeded features &amp; underground structure</h3>
    <p>Shared recipes place vegetation, natural formations, mineral deposits, attached crystals, and rare technological ruins. Stable feature identities and bounded neighbor queries keep placement independent of chunk loading order. Crystals and ruins are generated voxel content; they do not imply magical abilities, active machinery, loot, or progression systems.</p>
    <p>Fine cave noise combines with a regional underground plan for larger seeded cavern shapes and connections. Chunk generation and synchronous voxel queries use the same composition rules, so both agree on terrain, caves, and features at a given coordinate.</p>
    <figure>
        <iframe class="bloxels-world-video" src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/iklw2nn71pc?playsinline=1" title="Bloxels world generation preview" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share" allowfullscreen />
        <figcaption>World-generation presentation preview. This illustrates the generated world; runtime streaming is a separate system.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <h3>Reducing repeated column sampling</h3>
    <p>The demonstration below reduces repeated surface-height and biome sampling from <strong>4,096 block evaluations to 256 column evaluations</strong> in a 16³ chunk. Reusing each column’s samples means 16× fewer evaluations for this work, rather than a 16× speedup for the entire generator.</p>
    <CaseStudyMedia id="bloxels-sampling" media={bloxelsMedia.sampling} autoplayOnVisible playbackRate={0.5} />
</section>
<style>
    .bloxels-world-video { display: block; width: 100%; aspect-ratio: 16 / 9; border: 1px solid #263b50; background: #080e16; }
    .bloxels-biome-gallery { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 20px 14px; margin: 24px 0; }
    .bloxels-biome-gallery figure { min-width: 0; margin: 0; }
    .bloxels-biome-gallery figcaption strong { color: #d9e8f3; }
    @media (max-width: 600px) { .bloxels-biome-gallery { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); } }
</style>
<section class="case-section" aria-labelledby="bloxels-optimization">
    <h2 id="bloxels-optimization">Voxel Meshing</h2>
    <p>Hidden-face removal eliminates internal faces between occupied voxels. Greedy meshing then combines compatible exposed faces into larger quads. A shared axis-parameterized mesher handles the directional passes without maintaining separate implementations for each axis.</p>
    <BloxelsMeshComparison />
    <p>These counts measure geometry reduction on the demonstrated fixture, not an FPS improvement or a universal terrain count. In actual terrain, lighting gradients can prevent otherwise adjacent faces from merging.</p>
</section>
<section class="case-section" aria-labelledby="bloxels-streaming">
    <h2 id="bloxels-streaming">Terrain Rendering &amp; Streaming</h2>
    <p>The system manages the transition from generated data to visible, editable terrain. Immutable generation snapshots give workers plain configuration and registry data without depending on live Unreal objects. Nearby chunks and direct edits receive priority; queues, publication caps, and soft time budgets control generation, meshing, and main-thread submission.</p>
    <ol class="input-flow" aria-label="Terrain publication pipeline">
        {["Snapshot", "Generate & mesh", "Validate revision", "Publish", "Render-ready handoff"].map((step, index) => <li><span class="flow-step">{step}</span>{index < 4 && <span class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>}</li>)}
    </ol>
    <p>Allocation generations and mesh revisions reject stale worker results before they can replace newer terrain. Proven empty chunks retain readiness information without allocating full geometry or collision. Old visible representations remain in place until their replacements are ready to render.</p>
    <h3>Detail where it matters</h3>
    <p>Distant terrain uses directly sampled coarse volumetric sections selected around the player’s elevation, with farther surface approximations. Full and coarse generation share major underground shapes, while local edits invalidate the affected work rather than rebuilding the entire world.</p>
    <p>Reduced detail has deliberate limits: coarse volumetric terrain approximates small features and narrow openings, and farther surface-only levels omit cave interiors and tree/ruin silhouettes. Scheduling budgets are soft; individual uploads and collision work can still produce spikes.</p>
    <h3>Cached terrain rendering</h3>
    <p>Terrain retains procedural mesh section data and collision support, but uses a project-owned render proxy. Unchanged geometry registers draw submissions once so Unreal can cache main-pass, depth, and shadow commands instead of gathering procedural batches for every view. Edits and section replacement rebuild render state through the managed lifecycle.</p>
    <aside class="contribution-block" aria-label="Scoped coarse-generation benchmark">
        <h3>Local CPU result: coarse forest generation</h3>
        <p><strong>156.928 ms → 8.507 ms</strong> for 4× coarse generation of the same 64 × 64 × 128-block forest fixture.</p>
        <p>Recorded project measurement from a single local instrumented development run, comparing the earlier sampling path with direct coarse generation. This is a reduced-detail representation and CPU generation timing; it is not an FPS result or a promise of equivalent improvement in every world.</p>
    </aside>
</section>
<section class="case-section" aria-labelledby="bloxels-pathfinding">
    <h2 id="bloxels-pathfinding">Custom Voxel A*</h2>
    <p>Navigation derives traversal directly from the generated voxel world. A* evaluates neighbors on demand instead of precomputing a complete navigation-node map during generation, keeping that preparation work out of world loading.</p>
    <p>Traversal supports straight and diagonal movement, jumping up, and stepping down. A <strong>horizontal octile heuristic</strong> matches the straight and diagonal costs; vertical traversal adds movement cost. The path therefore reflects both voxel geometry and the character’s traversal rules.</p>
    <p>Path endpoint commands and a search visualization make candidate routes and traversal choices inspectable. The existing maze and raised-step demonstrations below show two different environments.</p>
    <div class="bloxels-path-comparison">
        <CaseStudyMedia id="bloxels-maze" media={bloxelsMedia.maze} autoplayOnVisible playbackRate={0.5} />
        <CaseStudyMedia id="bloxels-step" media={bloxelsMedia.step} autoplayOnVisible playbackRate={0.5} />
    </div>
</section>
<section class="case-section" aria-labelledby="bloxels-data">
    <h2 id="bloxels-data">Data &amp; Development Tools</h2>
    <p>Per-block DataAssets, FName-based identities, and a Voxel Registry subsystem provide shared block-definition lookup. Configurable generation recipes control biome profiles, palettes, vegetation, deposits, and structures; worker-safe snapshots carry the resolved data into generation tasks.</p>
    <p>A texture atlas and master material connect each block’s definition to its face textures through generated UVs, allowing block types to share a material. Debug commands and visualization expose generation and pathfinding behavior for inspection.</p>
    <h3>Reusable structures with .blxl</h3>
    <ol class="input-flow" aria-label="Structure export and import workflow">
        {["Select bounds", "Choose origin", "Export .blxl", "Import elsewhere"].map((step, index) => <li><span class="flow-step">{step}</span>{index < 3 && <span class="flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>}</li>)}
    </ol>
    <p>The selection system exports block coordinates relative to a chosen origin in a custom .blxl format, then imports that structure at another location. These tools make world composition and testing practical without adding a player-facing workflow for every experiment.</p>
    <p>Block placement/removal and a supporting hotbar/inventory exercise the editable terrain systems.</p>
</section>
